<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is Me! – Lesson # 1</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is Me!</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize that people are speaking in English. Interactive: Respond to cues in simple rhymes and songs.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify family-related words like "mom," "dad," or "me" when spoken (e.g., listening to and repeating family names, along with body movements while singing).
Can listen to and join in rhymes and songs about family (e.g., repeating and acting out words related to family members, such as "mother" and "father," while singing the rhymes).
Can identify names of family members when spoken (e.g., pointing to the father's picture when they hear the word "father").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h3></th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h3></th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h3></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song" using simple gestures. Then, the teacher shows a large picture flashcard of a happy family and points to it, saying, "Hello, family!" The teacher then points to himself/herself and says, "This is me!" while students listen and observe. This connects to the theme "This Is Me!" and activates basic understanding of "family."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces new vocabulary (mom, dad, baby, brother, sister) using large, colorful flashcards. For each card, the teacher holds it up, says the word clearly (e.g., "Mom!"), and performs a corresponding TPR action (e.g., for "mom," hug self; for "dad," make strong arm gesture; for "baby," rock arms). The teacher emphasizes the initial sounds, such as /m/ in "mom" and /s/ in "sister." The teacher repeats each word and action 2-3 times, encouraging students to listen and imitate the actions. The teacher also models "This is my mom." while pointing to the flashcard, introducing the possessive adjective.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding after modeling each word and action by asking simple questions and observing responses (pointing, nodding).
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the "mom" flashcard: "Who is this?" (Encourages students to listen and point or say "mom" if they can.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up the "baby" flashcard next to the "dad" flashcard: "Is this Dad or Baby?" (Students listen and point to the correct card.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An animated short song or chant titled "My Happy Family" (audio/visual input) displayed on a screen or large poster with simple illustrations of "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," and "baby." The song has a clear rhythm and repetitive lyrics like "Mom and Dad, Brother and Sister, Baby, too! This is my happy family, I love you!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Recognize that people are speaking in English and respond to cues in simple rhymes and songs about family.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening - First Play:</b> The teacher plays the "My Happy Family" song/chant once. Students listen and watch the video/poster, focusing on the rhythm and the pictures. The teacher encourages students to clap along or make simple gestures (e.g., hugging for "love").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Listening - Second Play:</b> The teacher plays the song/chant again. This time, the teacher holds up the corresponding flashcard (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby) as each family member is named in the song. Students listen carefully and point to the matching flashcard or illustration on the poster when they hear the word. The teacher models pointing.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Listening - Third Play:</b> The teacher plays the song a third time. Students listen for the names of family members. When they hear "mom," they point to the "mom" flashcard; when they hear "dad," they point to the "dad" flashcard, and so on. The teacher pauses the song briefly after each family member is named, prompting students with "Who is it?" and encouraging them to point or repeat the word (e.g., "mom!").</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for and identifying family member names (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby) and simple pronouns ("I", "my") and WH-questions ("Who is it?") in a controlled and semi-controlled environment. Focus on pronunciation of /m/, /s/, /p/, /b/ sounds, and respectful terms (Mom and Dad).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher lays out 5-6 flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby) on a mat or table. The teacher calls out a family member's name (e.g., "Mom!"). Students listen intently and point to the correct flashcard. The teacher reinforces by saying, "Yes, Mom!" and pointing as well. This activity focuses on identifying the initial sounds of words like /m/ in "mom" and /s/ in "sister."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Pick Up (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students sit in a circle with a set of family flashcards in front of them (or passed around). The teacher says, "Show me your sister!" or "Give me the baby!" Students listen and pick up or pass the correct flashcard. The teacher observes if students are listening and selecting the correct item. The teacher might ask, "Who is it?" when a student picks up a card, prompting a simple response like "Mom!" or "Dad!" (choral or individual attempts).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "My Family Says" (Interactive Listening Game):</b> The teacher leads a variation of "Simon Says" called "My Family Says." The teacher uses phrases like "My Family Says: Touch your head if you have a mom!" (all children touch their heads) or "My Family Says: Wave if you have a brother!" This integrates listening to longer phrases and associating them with familiar family concepts. The teacher focuses on clear pronunciation of /p/ in "play" (if a family member is playing in a picture) and /b/ in "brother."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Family Picture Show!" (Show and Tell, adapted for Pre-K). Students bring a small drawing or photo of one family member (or themselves/their pet, e.g., "dog," "cat" if part of their family). If no picture, they can draw one quickly on a provided paper or use a generic family flashcard.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in a circle, holding their picture/drawing.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models first: "This is my mom." (points to own picture). "I love my mom."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then goes to each student and asks, "Who is this?" (WH-question).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the question and respond by pointing to their picture and attempting to say the name (e.g., "Mom," "Dad," "Brother," or "Me!"). The teacher provides support and models the correct word if needed.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher can also ask, "Is this your dad?" (for confirmation) or "Do you love your mom?" (eliciting a nod/simple "Yes").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This task allows students to use vocabulary like "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," "baby," "dog," "cat," "me," and grammar like "my," "I," and responding to "Who is it?". It reinforces respecting family roles by using appropriate terms.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Family Picture Show" and "Listen and Point" activities.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will listen to the teacher say a family member's name and point to the correct flashcard or their own family picture. They will also attempt to repeat the word if prompted.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifies family members:</b> Can the student accurately point to "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," or "baby" when the teacher says the word? (Listening comprehension)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Responds to "Who is this?":</b> Can the student point to their picture and make an attempt to name the family member (e.g., "Mom," "Dad")? (Interactive listening and basic oral production)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Participation and engagement:</b> Did the student participate in the songs, chants, and pointing activities?</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of a happy face and a sad face.
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you listen to the family song and point to mom and dad? Show me happy face or sad face."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me your mom and dad? Happy face or sad face."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new family words did you hear today?" (Teacher can show flashcards as a reminder, eliciting pointing).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like the family song? (Thumbs up/down)."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., Students were engaged with the family song and TPR. Most students could identify 'mom' and 'dad' when spoken.)</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Need to provide more visual cues for 'brother' and 'sister' as some students confused them. Incorporate more repetition of initial sounds /b/ and /s/. Perhaps a simpler song or more direct flashcard interaction.)</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will use the same core family vocabulary (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby) in simple picture-text matching activities. Students will see the written words alongside the familiar pictures, building on their listening recognition to begin recognizing written forms. The next lesson will also reinforce phrases like "This is my mom" through matching sentences to pictures.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>